, with E= -33\u221a14 /28.\n\nWe can compute the equations of the tangents at B(0,0) and C(9,0).\n\nEquation of tangent at B: using formula, for point (x1,y1), the tangent is xx1 + yy1 + D(x +x1)/2 + E(y + y1)/2 + F=0.\n\nWait, for circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F=0, tangent at (x1,y1) is xx1 + yy1 + D(x +x1)/2 + E(y + y1)/2 + F=0.\n\nIn our case, circle equation is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -9x + Ey=0, so D=-9, E=E, F=0.\n\nSo tangent at B(0,0):\n\nx*0 + y*0 + (-9)(x +0)/2 + E(y +0)/2 +0=0.\n\nSimplify: (-9/2)x + (E/2)y=0.\n\nSimilarly, tangent at C(9,0):\n\nx*9 + y*0 + (-9)(x +9)/2 + E(y +0)/2=0.\n\nSimplify: 9x - (9(x +9))/2 + (E y)/2=0.\n\nMultiply through by 2: 18x -9x -81 + E y=0 => 9x + E y -81=0.\n\nSo, equations of tangents at B and C:\n\nAt B: (-9/2)x + (E/2)y=0.\n\nAt C: 9x + E y -81=0.\n\nThus, to find point D, solve these two equations.\n\nThus, from tangent at B:\n\n(-9/2)x + (E/2)y=0 => -9x + Ey=0 => Ey=9x.\n\nFrom tangent at C: 9x + Ey -81=0.\n\nSubstitute Ey=9x into tangent at C: 9x +9x -81=0 =>18x=81 =>x=81/18=9/2.\n\nThen, from Ey=9x: Ey=9*(9/2)=81/2.\n\nBut E= -33\u221a14 /28.\n\nThus, y= (81/2)/E= (81/2)/(-33\u221a14 /28)= (81/2)*(-28/(33\u221a14))= (81*(-14))/(33\u221a14).\n\nSimplify: 81 and 33 have GCD 3: 81/3=27, 33/3=11.\n\nThus, 27*(-14)/(11\u221a14)= -378/(11\u221a14).\n\nRationalize denominator: -378\u221a14/(11*14)= -378\u221a14 /154.\n\nSimplify 378/14=27, so -27\u221a14 /11.\n\nThus, y= -27\u221a14 /11.\n\nThus, point D is (9/2, -27\u221a14 /11).\n\nThus, coordinates of D are (9/2, -27\u221a14 /11).\n\nThus, now, to find AD.\n\nPoint A is at (1/2, 4\u221a14 /3).\n\nPoint D is at (9/2, -27\u221a14 /11).\n\nCompute distance AD.\n\nCompute differences:\n\n\u0394x=9/2 -1/2=8/2=4.\n\n\u0394y= (-27\u221a14 /11) - (4\u221a14 /3)= (-27\u221a14 /11 -4\u221a14 /3)= factor out \u221a14:\n\n\u221a14*(-27/11 -4/3)=\u221a14*(-81/33 -44/33)=\u221a14*(-125/33).\n\nThus, \u0394y= -125\u221a14 /33.\n\nThus, distance AD=\u221a[(4)^2 + (-125\u221a14 /33)^2 ].\n\nCompute 4^2=16.\n\nCompute (125\u221a14 /33)^2= (125^2 *14)/(33^2)= (15625*14)/1089=218750/1089.\n\nThus, distance AD=\u221a[16 +218750/1089].\n\nConvert 16 to 16*1089/1089=17424/1089.\n\nThus, AD=\u221a[(17424 +218750)/1089]=\u221a[236174/1089].\n\nSimplify 236174 \u00f71089.\n\nWait, 1089*217=235, 217*1089=235, 217*1000=217000, 217*89=19333, total 217000+19333=236333, which is more than 236174.\n\nWait, perhaps 217*1089=236, 217*1000=217000, 217*89=19333, so total 217*1089=236, 217*1089=236, 236, 236174-236174=0? Wait, no.\n\nWait, perhaps 1089*217=236, 236, 236174. Wait, 236174/1089=217.5.\n\nWait, 1089*217=236, 1089*217=236, 236, 236174-236, 236174-236*1089=236174-235, 235, 235*1089=236, 235*1089=236, 235*1000=235000, 235*89=20, 235*89=20, 235*89=20885, so 235*1089=235000+20885=255, 255, 255, 255, no, this is getting messy.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 236174/1089= approx 217.5.\n\nBut 1089=33\u00b2, so sqrt(236174/1089)=sqrt(236174)/33.\n\nWait, 236174: let's factor it.\n\n236174 \u00f72=118087.\n\n118087 \u00f77=16897.\n\n16897 \u00f713=1296.69, not integer.\n\nWait, perhaps 236174=2\u00d77\u00d716897.\n\nNot sure. Alternatively, perhaps 236174 is divisible by 14.\n\n236174 \u00f714=16897. So, 236174=14\u00d716897.\n\nThus, sqrt(236174)=sqrt(14\u00d716897). Hmm, 16897 is prime?\n\nNot sure, but perhaps not. Alternatively, perhaps 236174=2\u00d77\u00d716897.\n\nWait, 16897 \u00f77=2401. So 16897=7\u00d72401=7\u00d749\u00b2.\n\nThus, 236174=2\u00d77\u00d77\u00d749\u00b2=2\u00d77\u00d77\u00d749\u00b2=2\u00d749\u00b3.\n\nThus, sqrt(236174)=sqrt(2\u00d749\u00b3)=49\u00d7sqrt(2\u00d749)=49\u00d77\u00d7sqrt(2)=343\u221a2.\n\nThus, sqrt(236174)/33=343\u221a2 /33.\n\nThus, AD=343\u221a2 /33.\n\nWait, but 343=7\u00b3, 33=3\u00d711.\n\nThus, AP=AD=343\u221a2 /33.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that AP can be written as m/n where m and n are coprime. So, m=343\u221a2, n=33. But they have to be integers. So, I must have made a mistake.\n\nWait, no, my mistake was in computing the distance.\n\nWait, let me recast the problem.\n\nWe have point A at (1/2, 4\u221a14 /3).\n\nPoint D at (9/2, -27\u221a14 /11).\n\nCompute \u0394x=9/2 -1/2=8/2=4.\n\n\u0394y= -27\u221a14 /11 -4\u221a14 /3= (-27\u221a14 /11 -44\u221a14 /33)= (-81\u221a14 /33 -44\u221a14 /33)= (-125\u221a14)/33.\n\nThus, \u0394x=4, \u0394y= -125\u221a14 /33.\n\nThus, distance AD=\u221a(4\u00b2 + (125\u221a14 /33)^2 )=\u221a(16 + (15625\u00d714)/1089)=\u221a(16 +218750/1089).\n\nConvert 16 to 16\u00d71089/1089=17424/1089.\n\nThus, AD=\u221a(17424 +218750)/1089=\u221a(236174)/1089=\u221a(236174)/33.\n\nAs above, 236174=2\u00d77\u00d77\u00d749\u00b2=2\u00d749\u00b3.\n\nThus, sqrt(236174)=sqrt(2\u00d749\u00b3)=49\u00d7sqrt(2\u00d749)=49\u00d77\u00d7sqrt(2)=343\u221a2.\n\nThus, AD=343\u221a2 /33.\n\nBut the problem states AP can be written as m/n, m and n coprime integers. But 343\u221a2 is irrational, which contradicts.\n\nWait, so perhaps I made a mistake in the coordinates.\n\nWait, let me double-check the coordinates.\n\nFirst, triangle ABC with AB=5, BC=9, AC=10.\n\nCoordinates: Let me place point B at (0,0), point C at (9,0). Then point A is somewhere in the plane.\n\nCompute coordinates of A.\n\nUsing distance formula:\n\nAB=5: distance from A(x,y) to B(0,0)=5: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=25.\n\nAC=10: distance from A(x,y) to C(9,0)=10: (x-9)\u00b2 + y\u00b2=100.\n\nSubtract equations:\n\n(x-9)^2 + y^2 - (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2)=100 -25 => x\u00b2 -18x +81 + y\u00b2 -x\u00b2 -y\u00b2=75= -18x +81=75 => -18x= -6 => x=1/3.\n\nThus, x=1/3.\n\nThen, from AB=5: (1/3)^2 + y\u00b2=25 => y\u00b2=25 -1/9=224/9 => y=\u00b1(2\u221a14)/3.\n\nThus, point A is at (1/3, 2\u221a14 /3). So earlier, I think I had a sign error; it's 2\u221a14 /3, not 4\u221a14 /3. So coordinates of A are (1/3, 2\u221a14 /3).\n\nThen, coordinates of center O: from earlier, it's (9/2, 27\u221a14 /11). Wait, earlier when computing, I had the circle equation as x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -9x + something y - something=0.\n\nWait, let me recompute the center.\n\nThe perpendicular bisectors of AB and AC.\n\nMidpoint of AB: between B(0,0) and A(1/3,2\u221a14 /3): midpoint M1 is ((0 +1/3)/2, (0 +2\u221a14 /3)/2)= (1/6, \u221a14 /3).\n\nSlope of AB: from B(0,0) to A(1/3,2\u221a14 /3): slope is (2\u221a14 /3 -0)/(1/3 -0)= 2\u221a14.\n\nThus, perpendicular bisector slope: -1/(2\u221a14).\n\nEquation: passes through M1(1/6, \u221a14 /3): y - \u221a14 /3= (-1)/(2\u221a14)(x -1/6).\n\nSimilarly, midpoint of AC: between A(1/3,2\u221a14 /3) and C(9,0): midpoint M2 is ((1/3 +9)/2, (2\u221a14 /3 +0)/2)= (28/3 /2, \u221a14 /3)= (14/3, \u221a14 /3).\n\nSlope of AC: from A(1/3,2\u221a14 /3) to C(9,0): slope is (0 -2\u221a14 /3)/(9 -1/3)= (-2\u221a14 /3)/(26/3)= -2\u221a14 /26= -\u221a14 /13.\n\nThus, perpendicular bisector slope: reciprocal and opposite:13/\u221a14.\n\nEquation: passes through M2(14/3, \u221a14 /3): y -\u221a14 /3= (13/\u221a14)(x -14/3).\n\nNow, find intersection of two perpendicular bisectors.\n\nFirst equation: y= (-1)/(2\u221a14)(x -1/6) + \u221a14 /3.\n\nSecond equation: y= (13/\u221a14)(x -14/3) + \u221a14 /3.\n\nSet them equal:\n\n(-1)/(2\u221a14)(x -1/6) + \u221a14 /3 = (13/\u221a14)(x -14/3) + \u221a14 /3.\n\nSubtract \u221a14 /3 from both sides:\n\n(-1)/(2\u221a14)(x -1/6) = (13/\u221a14)(x -14/3).\n\nMultiply both sides by 2\u221a14:\n\n-1*(x -1/6) = 26*(x -14/3).\n\nSimplify:\n\n- x +1/6=26x - (26*14)/3.\n\n- x +1/6=26x -364/3.\n\nBring variables to left:\n\n- x -26x= -364/3 -1/6.\n\n-27x= - (364/3 +1/6)= - (1092/6 +1/6)= -1093/6.\n\nThus, x= (1093/6)/27=1093/(6*27)=1093/162.\n\nSimplify: 1093 is prime? Let me check: 1093 \u00f713=84.07, not integer. 1093 \u00f77=156.14, nope. 1093 \u00f73=364.333, nope. So x=1093/162.\n\nThen y: plug into first equation: y= (-1)/(2\u221a14)*(1093/162 -1/6) + \u221a14 /3.\n\nCompute inside: 1093/162 -1/6=1093/162 -27/162=1066/", "setting": "uniform4", "labels": "REEEERRRRREEERRRERRRRRRTTRRRRTRRERRTRRRRRRRRRREEERERTRRTRRRRRTRRTRTRRERTRTRRRRRRRRRRRERTR